Verified after: pgrep -fl myna.train -> empty. Nothing else was running.

## what an outside kill does NOT leave behind
runs/lc1-4k-s0/ holds model_last.pt + tokenizer.json only. The loop's guaranteed final save,
model.pt, and metrics.json are written after the step loop, so an external kill produces none of
them. The dose that ran is therefore read from the log (425 updates executed, last snapshot step
424, 830s elapsed at step 400), not from a metrics.json that does not exist.
The staged step-399 copy is the immutable witness: sha256 f389bde507f381c1fb0540d3095b552f26d539be2a6e1eb506b31419e49ca335
re-verified after the kill; runs/lc1-4k-s0/model_last.pt has since been overwritten by the step-424 save.

## ms column caveat
the eval ran at OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 nice 15 while the trainer kept using the box, so these ms are
2.3-2.9x the v0 curve's (128: 113.93 vs 59.5; 1024: 1318.74 vs 448.8) and are NOT comparable.
G4 is stated over accuracy (SPEC 2.2: >=0.90 at 4k, >=0.85 at 16k), so the throttle cannot change
the verdict; it can only make a latency claim dishonest, which is why it is not made.

## the differences between this curve and the published v0 curve, itemised
n 60 vs 16 (1 s.e. 0.0481 vs 0.0932); params_m 15.35 vs 14.45 (this is a cold-started model,
not v0's checkpoint); temperature 1.0 vs 3.0 is metadata only -- eval_needle.py:116 records
myna.temperature into the witness and never applies it to the score, so no accuracy can
differ on that axis; device cpu and seed 0 are the same. The ms column is additionally
throttled (above). So: same task, same generator, same harness, different checkpoint and
a 3.75x larger sample -- which is why this curve is reported beside the v0 one, not in place
of it and not as a decay measurement.

## the snapshot the kill left behind is resumable, and it says so
torch.load of runs/lc1-4k-s0/model_last.pt (the step-424 save): step 424, scheduler T_max 1200,
last_epoch 425, Adam per-parameter step 425, cfg vocab 4096. So the schedule was built for the
whole 1200-update dose and is only 425 in -- the reason this run stayed one continuous process
(1b's --resume restores T_max, so a resumed leg continues this cosine rather than rebuilding it).
If the owner asks for the full dose, --resume --steps 1200 is 775 more updates at the run's own
830 s / 400 updates = 2.075 s/update, i.e. ~27 minutes projected from a measured rate.
